# 4. Three-case state machine (bench/references.md §4), written as the
#    reference program actually says it, with a variant and a match:
#
#   λs:?. match s { Idle _ -> `Running {} | Running _ -> `Stopped {}
#                 | Stopped _ -> `Idle {} }
#
# Until 2026-08-29 this fixture faked the reference with a chain of
# equality tests on the numeric codes 0/1/2, because Phase 1 had no sum
# types. The Neovim denominator (151) was always computed for the match
# version, so the numerator was measuring a different program. It is not
# anymore.
#
# The parameter is annotated `?` and not `[Idle: {} | ...]` because a
# variant type is inferred and displayed, never spelled (DECISIONS.md,
# 2026-08-29): there is no variant syntax in `set-ann`, and a match on a
# `?` scrutinee is exhaustive because the requirement is empty. The three
# cases come into being as arms — `add-arm` is the only way to mint a
# constructor — and the injections in the bodies aim at those same three
# identities.

# λs:?. ⦇⦈
construct-lam
move-parent
rename s
move-child 0

# match s {}, then one arm per state
construct-var s
construct-match
add-arm
rename-constructor Idle
add-arm
rename-constructor Running
add-arm
rename-constructor Stopped

# Idle -> `Running {}
move-parent
move-child 1
construct-inj
move-parent
set-constructor Running
move-child 0
construct-record
remove-field

# Running -> `Stopped {}
move-parent
move-next-sibling
construct-inj
move-parent
set-constructor Stopped
move-child 0
construct-record
remove-field

# Stopped -> `Idle {}
move-parent
move-next-sibling
construct-inj
move-parent
set-constructor Idle
move-child 0
construct-record
remove-field
